{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועשה חסד", :translit "ve'asah chesed", :gloss "and showing lovingkindness"} {:he "ועשה חסד למשיחו לו", :translit "ve'asah chesed limshicho lo", :gloss "and He showed lovingkindness to His anointed, to him"} {:he "ויאמר דוד אעשה חסד עם חנון", :translit "vayomer david e'eseh chesed im chanun", :gloss "and David said: I will show lovingkindness with Hanun"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["ועשה חסד"]}], :frame {:text "The principle that one who shows lovingkindness to others receives lovingkindness in return is illustrated through David. David initiated acts of kindness even toward non-Jews, as in 2 Samuel 9:1 where ויאמר דוד אעשה חסד עם חנון (there read as חנון, showing kindness with one who is gracious). Consequently, God showed lovingkindness to David, fulfilling 2 Samuel 22:51: ועשה חסד למשיחו למשיחו לדוד — though here cited as למשיחו לו. The phrase ועשה חסד appears twice in the Decalogue (Exodus 20:6 and Deuteronomy 5:10) and twice concerning David.", :sources ["2 Samuel 9:1" "2 Samuel 22:51" "Exodus 20:6" "Deuteronomy 5:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ולשומרי מצותי", :translit "ulshomrei mitzvotai", :gloss "and those who keep My commandments"} {:he "לא תשא", :translit "lo tisa", :gloss "you shall not take"} {:he "שבע תועבות בלבו", :translit "shivah to'evot belibo", :gloss "seven abominations in his heart"} {:he "ז' עונותיכם מלאים", :translit "shivah avonoteichem m'le'im", :gloss "seven your iniquities are full"} {:he "ז' שמות ליצה\"ר", :translit "shivah shemot leyotzer hara", :gloss "seven names for the evil inclination"} {:he "ז' מדרגות לגיהנם", :translit "shivah madregot ligeihinom", :gloss "seven rungs of Gehinnom"} {:he "כזב", :translit "kazav", :gloss "falsehood"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["כזב"]} {:kind :other, :note "Seven crowns (tagin) on the shin of לא תשא correspond to seven-fold punishment lists."}], :frame {:text "The adjacency of ולשומרי מצותי to לא תשא teaches the severity of an oath in vain. This is a standard form of rabbinic inference from textual juxtaposition (semikhut parshiyot). The second interpretation, that one must perform commandments לשמן (for their own sake) and not שלא לשמן (not for their own sake), reflects the principle found in m. Nedarim 9a regarding לשמה. The verse ז' עונותיכם מלאים appears in Proverbs 5:22 (though commonly understood as referring to cords of sin). Seven abominations in the heart derives from Proverbs 26:25: כי שבע תועבות בלבו. Seven names for the evil inclination is a known rabbinic tradition (cf. Sukkah 52a-b, which lists several names). Seven rungs of Gehinnom reflects the tradition in Eruvin 19a that Gehinnom has seven names.", :sources ["Proverbs 26:25" "Sukkah 52a" "Eruvin 19a" "m. Nedarim 9a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["ועשה חסד"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ועשה חסד", :refs ["Genesis 24:12" "Exodus 20:6" "Deuteronomy 5:10" "2 Samuel 22:51" "Psalms 18:51"], :n 5, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 7, :occurrences ["כזב"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "כזב", :refs ["Isaiah 28:15" "Isaiah 28:17" "Ezekiel 13:6" "Ezekiel 13:7" "Ezekiel 13:8" "Ezekiel 13:9" "Ezekiel 13:19" "Ezekiel 21:34"], :n 22, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Seven crowns (tagin) on the shin of לא תשא correspond to seven-fold punishment lists.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:17:44.201369181Z", :model "glm-5.2"}